ids.txt: ideographic description sequences, one character to a line ("character<TAB>sequence"),
taken from BabelStone's IDS.TXT (https://babelstone.co.uk/CJK/IDS.TXT, Unicode 16.0, file date
2025-06-27, maintained by Andrew West). For each character the sequence for its G (China) source
form is kept, or the first sequence if none is marked G; characters described by themselves (no
decomposition) are left out. The "^", "$" and source markers are removed.

The file's terms: "anyone is free to make use of the IDS data provided in this file for personal or
commercial purposes without asking permission or providing attribution. I furthermore waive any
copyright claims to the presentation format of the IDS data used in this file, so anyone is free to
fork the file, or use it as the basis for a new IDS data set."

Unencoded components appear as {n} (see the original file for what each is); "？" stands for a
component that cannot be represented.
